WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.215 --> 00:00:05.788
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.788 --> 00:00:08.234
What's in a name?

00:00:08.234 --> 00:00:12.896
A rose by any other name
would smell just as sweet,

00:00:12.896 --> 00:00:16.679
Shakespeare famously said via Juliet.

00:00:16.679 --> 00:00:20.103
It's difficult to disagree
with Shakespeare, but

00:00:20.103 --> 00:00:23.854
I have to respectfully disagree
with him on this matter.

00:00:23.854 --> 00:00:27.834
The name of a rose or
of any other thing or being for

00:00:27.834 --> 00:00:33.286
that matter has a direct implication
on its smell and so much more.

00:00:33.286 --> 00:00:38.386
My journey for what I nowadays
call global nominal equity,

00:00:38.386 --> 00:00:45.424
nominal, not in the sense of interest
rates, but nominal as it relates to names,

00:00:45.424 --> 00:00:50.237
started at a very cold autumn
afternoon in New England.

00:00:50.237 --> 00:00:56.166
This was my first year in the United
States, my freshman year in college.

00:00:56.166 --> 00:01:01.660
And here I was waiting for a callback
audition at small theater in town.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:05.652
First, five minutes past, Chris went in.

00:01:05.652 --> 00:01:08.026
I sat and watched others.

00:01:08.026 --> 00:01:11.568
At the ten minutes mark,
it was Chuck who went in.

00:01:11.568 --> 00:01:14.551
At the 15 minutes mark, it was Clayton.

00:01:14.551 --> 00:01:17.599
I stood up and
went to the audition manager and asked,

00:01:17.599 --> 00:01:19.514
is there a delay in the schedule?

00:01:19.514 --> 00:01:20.981
And she said, what's your name?

00:01:20.981 --> 00:01:24.410
I said it's Cengiz,
spelled out C-E-N-G-I-Z.

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:27.495
She said,
I don't see a name like that here.

00:01:27.495 --> 00:01:31.152
You must have been waitlisted and
I'm sorry nobody dropped out, so

00:01:31.152 --> 00:01:32.444
better luck next time.

00:01:32.444 --> 00:01:34.425
Dylan, Dylan you're up next.

00:01:34.425 --> 00:01:38.968
Confused and dejected, my 18 year
old self went back to the seats and

00:01:38.968 --> 00:01:43.519
watched a certain Dylan pass by
excitedly for his callback audition.

00:01:43.519 --> 00:01:47.650
I just wondered, why did they not
tell me that I was waitlisted?

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:50.087
Why would they waste my and their time?

00:01:50.087 --> 00:01:54.441
I just sat there, thinking and
hoping that something would happen,

00:01:54.441 --> 00:01:59.411
that Cameron would turn into Cengiz,
that Christian would turn into Cengiz.

00:01:59.411 --> 00:02:02.332
Nothing turned into Cengiz.

00:02:02.332 --> 00:02:05.290
Well, it would take me three years and

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:09.737
two months to figure out
exactly what happened that day.

00:02:09.737 --> 00:02:14.788
My enterprising future self
would do some sleuthing,

00:02:14.788 --> 00:02:20.055
would go in and find out
the audition list from that day and

00:02:20.055 --> 00:02:25.214
find that at 2:45 PM, October 29th, 2014,

00:02:25.214 --> 00:02:30.190
my name was indeed penned as Ching,
C-H-I-N-G.

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:34.265
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Come on,

00:02:34.265 --> 00:02:39.440
it's not even similar,
it could not have been.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:43.918
I kept thinking, what other things
were lost to me, unknowingly,

00:02:43.918 --> 00:02:48.885
simply because somebody could not
remember, pronounce or spell my name?

00:02:48.885 --> 00:02:51.296
What other things were lost?

00:02:51.296 --> 00:02:55.946
So it has since then I became
emboldened daily, in both quiet and

00:02:55.946 --> 00:03:00.939
loud ways, for advocates for
now what I call Global Nominal Equity,

00:03:00.939 --> 00:03:06.018
meaning we should treat each name
with the respect that it deserves and

00:03:06.018 --> 00:03:10.497
we make the appropriate
accommodations that we pronounce and

00:03:10.497 --> 00:03:13.801
spell everyone's name always everywhere.

00:03:13.801 --> 00:03:17.467
Well, if you haven't heard of Global
Nominal Equity before, you're not alone.

00:03:17.467 --> 00:03:21.213
Because I just took the creative license
to make it up a couple of months ago.

00:03:21.213 --> 00:03:24.314
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And now it's our turn, I'm sharing it

00:03:24.314 --> 00:03:29.769
with you so that we can co-own it,
co-co-own it, and co-create it together.

00:03:29.769 --> 00:03:35.325
Well, my life has been structured by
my name for a very long time like,

00:03:35.325 --> 00:03:39.104
actually, all the rest
of you in the audience.

00:03:39.104 --> 00:03:42.691
On a good day, somebody who meets me for
the first time will say,

00:03:42.691 --> 00:03:44.359
how do I pronounce your name?

00:03:44.359 --> 00:03:48.415
I will say Cengiz with a smile,
they will say Cengiz with a smile and

00:03:48.415 --> 00:03:53.417
they will continue to pronounce it without
hesitation with a smile in their eyes,

00:03:53.417 --> 00:03:55.683
with positive feelings all around.

00:03:55.683 --> 00:04:00.092
On a bad day, however, somebody who
meets me for the first time will say,

00:04:00.092 --> 00:04:01.884
how do I pronounce your name?

00:04:01.884 --> 00:04:06.383
I will say Cengiz and they will say,
this sounds very difficult,

00:04:06.383 --> 00:04:10.904
I think I will butcher it, so I'm scared,
I'm just not going to say it.

00:04:10.904 --> 00:04:13.481
And then I'll say,
it's not that difficult.

00:04:13.481 --> 00:04:16.636
Come on, well, it's two syllables
actually, it's Cen and giz.

00:04:16.636 --> 00:04:21.102
There's plenty of bottles and now if these
bring a historical connection by saying it

00:04:21.102 --> 00:04:24.275
comes from [INAUDIBLE] so
that it gets anchored in your minds.

00:04:24.275 --> 00:04:26.833
Well, the answer it's about their member,

00:04:26.833 --> 00:04:30.141
their answer continues saying
first of all, who is that?

00:04:30.141 --> 00:04:30.990
I don't know.

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:34.167
And second of all, it's really difficult.

00:04:34.167 --> 00:04:35.774
I'll just pass this one out,

00:04:35.774 --> 00:04:39.840
to just proceed to interact with me
without mentioning my name ever again.

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:44.460
And potentially forgetting many things
that we talked about except one

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:47.470
small fact that my name
was unpronounceable.

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:50.019
Well, this doesn't only happen to me.

00:04:50.019 --> 00:04:54.571
In the broader Anglo Saxon universe,
it also happens to Mailey,

00:04:54.571 --> 00:04:58.229
Yuvgagne, Agnieszka,
Dierdre, and Adebouale.

00:04:58.229 --> 00:05:00.833
But we are all perpetrators of this.

00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:04.859
The number of times I said
Margots instead of Margeau or

00:05:04.859 --> 00:05:09.234
Jeanne instead of Jean before
I was corrected that he's not

00:05:09.234 --> 00:05:12.752
a character from Aladdin
was just one too many.

00:05:12.752 --> 00:05:17.735
And that is why I believe that we
should make sure that we do not

00:05:17.735 --> 00:05:22.039
provide blatant commentary
on the most personal and

00:05:22.039 --> 00:05:27.151
intimate yet public thing that
we all have, our first names.

00:05:27.151 --> 00:05:31.717
That is exactly why today I chose to
talk to you about the notion of having

00:05:31.717 --> 00:05:36.436
an unpronounceable names and what should
we do to ensure that we treat each

00:05:36.436 --> 00:05:40.487
name respectfully and
make the appropriate accommodations.

00:05:40.487 --> 00:05:44.956
One's name, the sound of which,
the spelling of which,

00:05:44.956 --> 00:05:49.609
the shape of every letter of
which to dramatize a little bit,

00:05:49.609 --> 00:05:54.081
has a direct impact on our existence,
our self ideation,

00:05:54.081 --> 00:06:00.398
our public perception, our chance of
leaving a legacy, our past and our future.

00:06:00.398 --> 00:06:04.864
Many people we know of went to great
lengths to change their names so

00:06:04.864 --> 00:06:08.076
that they could make
them more pronounceable.

00:06:08.076 --> 00:06:14.771
Issur Danielovitch became Kirk Douglas,
Natalie Hershlag became Natalie Portman,

00:06:14.771 --> 00:06:20.642
Tont Woods became Tiger Woods and
Robert Allen Zimmerman became Bob Dylan,

00:06:20.642 --> 00:06:26.621
and he in fact famously said, some people
are just born with the wrong names.

00:06:26.621 --> 00:06:30.213
I disagree with him too after
disagreeing with Shakespeare.

00:06:30.213 --> 00:06:36.648
Well, actually, research has shown
many things about this front.

00:06:36.648 --> 00:06:38.334
Research shows five things.

00:06:38.334 --> 00:06:42.675
One, that people with unpronounceable
names have more difficulty forming

00:06:42.675 --> 00:06:44.110
intimate connections.

00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:46.227
They have more difficulty, two,

00:06:46.227 --> 00:06:51.194
they have more difficulty leaving a
lasting impression during a job interview.

00:06:51.194 --> 00:06:56.264
Three, they have difficulty having
their name being remembered.

00:06:56.264 --> 00:06:59.686
Four, they perform worse at school and
five,

00:06:59.686 --> 00:07:04.531
they have a smaller chances of
being an elected public official.

00:07:04.531 --> 00:07:06.701
Yes, even that.

00:07:06.701 --> 00:07:10.730
Unpronounceable names are more
likely to be misremembered and

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:13.319
mispronounced even after correction.

00:07:13.319 --> 00:07:19.483
And with each mispronunciation, slightly
altering our self perceived identity,

00:07:19.483 --> 00:07:25.051
our already complicated existence,
our foreignness and our difference.

00:07:25.051 --> 00:07:30.150
And that is why it is up to our hands
to ensure that we do not introduce

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:35.710
further micro-inequalities into our
already deeply unequal world and

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:38.442
take up the matter into our hands and

00:07:38.442 --> 00:07:42.815
find a way in which we treat
everyone with a challenging or

00:07:42.815 --> 00:07:49.042
an easy name with the same willingness
to remember, excitement and curiosity.

00:07:49.042 --> 00:07:52.263
Well, you might ask, how do we do that?

00:07:52.263 --> 00:07:58.106
It actually starts very simple,
with a very, very simple shift.

00:07:58.106 --> 00:07:59.573
It's a mental shift.

00:07:59.573 --> 00:08:04.487
It starts with realizing one thing,
and that is that no name is

00:08:04.487 --> 00:08:10.078
actually unpronounceable,
it's a little bit dull, but it is dull.

00:08:10.078 --> 00:08:12.953
There are no names that
are unpronounceable.

00:08:12.953 --> 00:08:16.379
If somebody chose to name
their child something,

00:08:16.379 --> 00:08:19.320
this means they were able to pronounce it.

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:22.790
That also means you too,
after not too many attempts,

00:08:22.790 --> 00:08:24.686
will be able to pronounce it.

00:08:24.686 --> 00:08:29.375
I promise, if you cannot pronounce
it right, they will correct it.

00:08:29.375 --> 00:08:34.139
The key is to lean into it,
not to shy away from it.

00:08:34.139 --> 00:08:39.899
There is a lot of shame in not trying,
but there's no shame in trying.

00:08:39.899 --> 00:08:43.565
So the next time when you come
across a name that you immediately

00:08:43.565 --> 00:08:47.854
deem to be relatively complicated and
unpronounceable, take a second,

00:08:47.854 --> 00:08:51.813
think about it in your mind,
rehearse it, and then say it out loud.

00:08:51.813 --> 00:08:53.990
And then ask the person,
did I say it right?

00:08:53.990 --> 00:08:55.863
If you didn't, they will correct it.

00:08:55.863 --> 00:09:01.623
And after not too many attempts,
you will ultimately get it right.

00:09:01.623 --> 00:09:03.938
Well, it's sometimes
a little bit more difficult.

00:09:03.938 --> 00:09:06.215
So what you'll have to do,
my recommendation,

00:09:06.215 --> 00:09:08.876
is that lean into it a bit more and
then try to use the name,

00:09:08.876 --> 00:09:11.889
actually refer to the person with
the name without omitting it.

00:09:11.889 --> 00:09:15.293
So every time you're actually trying
to get that person's attention,

00:09:15.293 --> 00:09:19.027
say the name again and again and again so
that it gets anchored in your mind and for

00:09:19.027 --> 00:09:22.303
bonus points, you can even ask what
it means and where it comes from.

00:09:22.303 --> 00:09:26.921
Well, it is time that we make
every name pronounceable,

00:09:26.921 --> 00:09:32.612
not by changing our names but
by changing our attitudes towards them.

00:09:32.612 --> 00:09:37.159
We need to ensure that we live
in a world in which everybody

00:09:37.159 --> 00:09:40.684
feels equally seen and equally respected.

00:09:40.684 --> 00:09:44.730
It is time that we,
the ones with unpronounceable names,

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:50.112
have to stop carrying the burden of
others inability to pronounce our names.

00:09:50.112 --> 00:09:54.837
So to build that equitable world,
let's all commit ourselves to making

00:09:54.837 --> 00:09:59.486
every word that's seemingly
unpronounceable, pronounceable, and

00:09:59.486 --> 00:10:04.612
with that, every person that is
seemingly foreign, intricately familiar.

00:10:04.612 --> 00:10:09.637
And to do so, let's commit
ourselves to now our co-owned and

00:10:09.637 --> 00:10:13.218
co-coined term, Global Nominal Equity.

00:10:13.218 --> 00:10:15.461
Thank you
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:10:15.461 --> 00:10:20.204
[MUSIC]

